<seg id="s0003" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">In July, prednisone and triazolam were discontinued 
and the opsoclonus recurred. Drugs were restarted with 
steady improvement of the opsoclonus, rigidity, and 
painful muscle spasms. By January 1993, eye move- 
ments were normal and the patient was able to walk 
with a walker and to use the left upper limb when driv- 
ing her wheel-chair. A new extensive work-up including 
a needle biopsy of several small breast nodules was nega- 
tive. Triazolam was discontinued and prednisone tapered 
to 20 mg/d. The patient remained stable until June 1993, 
when she died from a pulmonary thromboembolism. 
Permission for autopsy was denied.</seg> 
Discussion. Luque et a12 reported a paraneoplastic 
neurologic syndrome of ataxia and opsoclonus in 
eight women with anti-Ri autoantibodies; six had 
breast cancer. The antibodies were not present in 
patients with paraneoplastic opsoclonus associated 
with neuroblastoma or small-cell lung cancer, nor 
in patients with breast cancer without opsoclonus 
or in patients with nonparaneoplastic opsoclonus. 
Anti-Ri antibodies could then be a useful marker 
for an underlying breast cancer. Our patient pre- 
sented with severe ataxia, myoclonus, opsoclonus, 
and high titers of anti-Ri antibodies. However, 
repeat examinations failed to disclose a neoplasm 
up to her death 20 months later. There are 
reports*,4 of two similar patients. Although the pos- 
sibility of an underlying cancer cannot be ruled out, 
these patients may represent a subset of anti- 
Ri-positive cases of nonparaneoplastic origin. A 
similar situation occurs in the Lambert-Eaton 
myasthenic syndrome, where only 50% of patients 
have a small-cell lung ~ancer.~ 
The symptoms of anti-Ri-associated paraneo- 
plastic syndromes may fluctuate spontaneously or 
under treatment, suggesting a functional rather 
than an irreversible damage to the  neuron^.^-^ In 
our patient, improvement to the opsoclonus- 
myoclonus syndrome (OMS) with triazolam and 
prednisone was followed by recurrence when the 
treatment was discontinued. As in other  patient^,^,^ 
although spontaneous remissions may occur, the 
improvement of our patient’s symptoms with medi- 
cation suggests a positive response to treatment. 
Besides the OMS, our patient presented with 
progressive tonically maintained rigidity and 
painful spasms evoked by sensory or emotional 
stimuli of the left limbs, together with piloerection, 
flushing, and hyperventilation. These symptoms 
are typical of a syndrome of PEM,6--s which has 
been associated with a small-cell carcinoma of the 
lung in at least one ~atient.~ 
The exact anatomic substrate of OMS and PEM is 
unknown. Although the MRI of our patient disclosed 
a small lesion in the pons, we believe the lesion was 
an old ischemic infarct, which possibly explains the 
patient’s residual left hemiparesis. In PEM, changes 
in the lower brainstem and spinal cord have been 
present in both MRIs and at autopsy.6 Similarly, the 
spectrum of manifestations in anti-Ri-positive 
patients suggests predominant involvement of the 
brainstem, and in a postmortem study, the brain 
showed perivascular and parenchymal inflammatory 
infiltrates particularly involving the tegmentum of 
the pons and mesen~ephalon.~ Lastly, the gene 
encoding an antigen recognized by anti-Ri-positive 
sera is preferentially expressed in the ventral brain- 
stem and spinal cord of mice.1° The present case 
report suggests that patients with anti-Ri antibodies 
develop a brainstem encephalitis manifested clini- 
cally with opsoclonus, myoclonus, ataxia, and PEM. 
